$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186713906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "67bd1b3f-95bc-5748-9f9e-a99a37f7eaa3" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad \\mathbf{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad \\mathbf{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind bases of $S=\\operatorname{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ by inspection. **Select every set that is a basis of $S$.**" :question/worked-solution "A **basis** must be linearly independent and span $S$.\n\nIf $\\mathbf{a}_{2}=t\\mathbf{a}_{3}$, coordinate $2$ would force $t=0$. But coordinate $1$ would then give $(0)(0)=0\\ne 1$. Thus these two nonzero vectors are not parallel and are linearly independent.\n\nTo have $\\mathbf{a}_{1}=x\\mathbf{a}_{3}+y\\mathbf{a}_{2}$, coordinates $2$ and $1$ force $x=-2$ and $y=0$. The remaining coordinate would have to be $(-2)(-1)+(0)(-1)=2$; the displayed value is $2$.\n\nChecking the proposed combination gives\n\n$$\n(-2)\\mathbf{a}_{3}+(0)\\mathbf{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\mathbf{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nRemoving $\\mathbf{a}_{1}$ leaves the span unchanged. The parallel pair $\\{\\mathbf{a}_{1},\\mathbf{a}_{3}\\}$ is dependent. Pairing either nonzero vector on that line with $\\mathbf{a}_{2}$ gives an independent spanning pair. No singleton spans the two independent directions, and the triple is dependent.\n\nTherefore, select exactly these bases:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{B}_{1}&=\\{\\mathbf{a}_{1},\\mathbf{a}_{2}\\} \\\\ \\mathcal{B}_{2}&=\\{\\mathbf{a}_{2},\\mathbf{a}_{3}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4ce4aaf-dfc5-5bf7-94c8-8b9fe78dfdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ide